# Score engraving audit

A read-only check that turns "is this score fit to share?" into a list of
findings. It needs only `python3` (standard library); it does not need this
repository or any score editor.

```bash
python3 "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/audit_musicxml.py" score.xml          # text
python3 "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/audit_musicxml.py" score.xml --json   # for scripts
```

Exit status is 1 when there is an `error`, 2 when the file cannot be read.
It reads `.xml`, `.musicxml` and `.mxl`.

## Severities

- `error`: fix before sharing (no title, generic part names, mostly padding).
- `warn`: probably wrong; compare with the source print.
- `info`: a fact, not a defect.

## What it checks

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `title-missing`, `title-case`, `title-whitespace`, `title-numbered` | Title absent, all lower/upper case, padded, or carrying a list number |
| `composer-missing`, `rights-missing` | No composer/credit text, no copyright/source line |
| `credits-split` | A credit block (composer + arranger, multi-line footer) stored one line per credit; fix with `score-metadata-repair --merge-credits` |
| `part-name-generic`, `part-abbreviation-missing` | `Voice0`-style names; no short names for later systems |
| `beams-mixed` | A beat group where some notes are beamed and others are not |
| `beams-some-unbeamed`, `beams-none` | Unbeamed groups (see the beaming note below) |
| `beam-style` | Info: the beamed groups look syllabic or modern, or "undetermined" (see below) |
| `stems-wrong-direction` | Stems against the usual rule (needs `<stem>` elements; info when there are none) |
| `lyrics-crowded` | Bars where lyric syllables will probably collide, worst bars listed |
| `chords-as-text`, `chords-lowercase` | Chord symbols stored as text; `c` where `C` was meant |
| `lyrics-unjoined` | Every syllable is its own word, so no hyphens will print |
| `padding-bars`, `clefs-after-bar1` | OMR padding and redundant clefs; fix with `omr-score-repair` |
| `layout-no-breaks` | No system or page breaks; choose them by phrase after import |

## Beaming: what is and is not a defect

A MusicXML file with **no** `<beam>` elements is not reliably beamed on
import: the Silent Night import beamed by meter, but the Brumel one (2026-11-05,
four-part 2/2, 187 eighths) came out with every eighth flagged while the source
print beamed them. So `beams-none` is informational only after you have
compared with the source; if the print beams, run `beaming-repair --scope all`.
Sibelius keeps **explicit** beams, so a partial beam from an OMR scan stays
wrong: in the Silent Night scan, bars 1 and 2 (6/8) beam a dotted eighth with
the sixteenth and leave the third eighth flagged. `beams-mixed` finds these.

Beams are a style choice for vocal music. Syllabic editions break beams at
each syllable change, so compare with the source print before treating a
`beams-mixed` or `beams-some-unbeamed` finding as an error. The `beam-style`
finding reports which style the already-beamed groups follow. Also note that a
file exported by an editor can show few `<beam>` elements and still print
correctly: judge those by the rendered PDF, not by the XML.

### Beam style (`beam-style`, info)

Feeds the `--style` choice of the `beaming-repair` skill. It looks at pairs of
neighbouring beamable notes inside one beat (meter group) where the second
note starts a new syllable: a joined pair is evidence for **modern** (beamed
by the beat regardless of syllables, `--style meter`), a broken pair is
evidence for **syllabic** (`--style syllabic --scope all`). At least about 6
beamed groups are needed, otherwise it reports "undetermined", as it does for
mixed evidence (neither side at least 70 percent) or when no beamed group has
a new syllable inside a beat. Broken pairs can also be beams the scan missed,
so confirm with the source print before choosing.

## Stems (`stems-wrong-direction`)

Only for files that keep `<stem>` elements (OMR output); editor exports
have none and get an info saying stems were not checked. The clef (G2, F4, C3,
C4 and octave-change) gives the staff position. On a staff with one voice in
the bar, a note on or above the middle line stems down and below it up;
a chord is decided by its note farthest from the middle line, and so is a
beamed group (Gould), so mixed directions inside a group are ignored. A note on
the middle line, or a chord equally far above and below, is not judged. With
two voices on the staff, voice 1 stems up and voice 2 down; three or more
voices are skipped. A staff whose stems all point one way (at least 12) is
treated as a deliberate open-score style and skipped, and named in the message.
It warns only when at least 3 and 5 percent of the units are wrong.

## Crowded lyrics (`lyrics-crowded`)

MusicXML has no layout, so collisions are estimated. For each voice and bar,
every syllable counts its characters plus 1 for a trailing hyphen (the widest
verse at that note when verses are stacked). A bar is flagged when

- a syllable of 4 or more characters sits on a note shorter than an eighth, or
- characters per beat exceed 7 (5.5 when the voice has 3 or more verses, because
  stacked verses also collide with the staff below in tight systems).

The beat count is the larger of the stated meter and the notes actually in the
bar (OMR bars often outgrow a wrong time signature). Bars are ranked by
long-syllable count and density; the worst five are in `where`. It is a
warning to look at the rendered PDF, not proof of a collision.

## Using the result

1. Run the audit on the OMR output, before importing.
2. Fix what is fixable in the XML (`omr-score-repair`, `lyric-hyphenation`).
3. Import, fix the rest in the editor, export a PDF and compare it with the
   source scan.
4. Run the audit again on the re-exported MusicXML; no count should get worse.

## Copyright line templates

`rights-missing` asks for one of three lines. Ask the user for the source and
its rights status; never add a © to a transcription unprompted.

1. Public-domain source: `Public domain. Transcribed from [source] for rehearsal use.`
2. In-copyright source: carry the original notice, then
   `Transcribed for rehearsal use only. Not for distribution.`
3. Our own arrangement: `Arrangement © [year] [name].`
